+## Chrome Browser Session Workflow
+
+Use Chrome when an authenticated site requires Chrome-specific behavior,
+extensions, or signing software. Chrome can execute DOM JavaScript through
+Apple Events without bringing its window forward, but the user must first turn
+on:
+
+```text
+View -> Developer -> Allow JavaScript from Apple Events
+```
+
+Enumerate window and tab IDs, resolve the intended URL from sanitized output,
+and pin every later read or click to both exact IDs. Do not use `activate`, the
+frontmost window, tab positions, or screen-coordinate clicks. Start with a
+non-mutating `document.title`, `location.origin`, and `document.readyState`
+smoke test before page-specific work.
+
+Chrome automation has two modes. Silent mode is the default: resolve the exact
+`targetWindow` and `targetTab` and execute DOM JavaScript directly on that tab.
+DOM reads, form events, and element clicks do not require selecting the tab or
+raising Chrome. Visible/handoff mode is allowed only when the user explicitly
+asks to open or show the page, work in their current tab, bring it forward, or
+prepare it for manual interaction. Only that explicit mode may activate Chrome
+or select the resolved tab; silent mode must not use `activate`, `set active
+tab`, `set index of window`, or System Events UI scripting.
+
+Chrome keeps the authenticated session. Never extract cookies, browser storage,
+authorization headers, or tokens for use by local tools. Keep DOM results
+bounded, write raw Apple Events output only to private task-scoped `.temp/`
+files, sanitize it with `mac-document-sanitize`, and inspect only the sanitized
+copy. See
+[`chrome-session.md`](agents/skills/mac-infra/references/chrome-session.md) for
+exact AppleScript examples and the interaction safety contract.
